\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why, as to the company, that was remarkable enough!\u8221\'94 said I, quaffing off a tumbler of champagne, to assist my invention.}
\par\pard\plain \sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0  his eyes starting from their sockets, and his mouth growing wider every moment, as he listed with the most intense interest}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f2\fs20\b0\i0 It simpers at us out of a graceful vignette, or peeps through the fantastical wreaths of a decorative capital; draped in Oriental robes, surmounted with a Turkish head-dress, the scalp despoiled by Mahomedan razor of all its flowing honours, save one tress upon its summit, the beard trimmed in conformity with the latest fashion of Stamboul. Turn the page and behold him galloping, with drawn yataghan, and glowing with military ardour, in the suite of a prince of Lebanon, out for a foray amongst the Maronites. A little farther he languishes at the feet of the lovely daughter of a Druse Sheik, or exerts his influence\hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 far from inconsiderable\hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 to obtain the release of her captive parent. But however occupied, whether martially, amorously, or philanthropically, Gerard de Nerval is always before us, the principal figure upon his own canvass. He is nothing if not egotistical. As to the value and extent of his information concerning the countries he visited, it is impossible to rate them highly, since he admits that his knowledge of Arabic was for some time limited to the single word\~}{\f3\fs20\b0\i1 tayeb\hich\f3 \emdash \loch\f3 it is good}{\f2\fs20\b0\i0 \hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 which conveys, he says, an infinity of meanings, according to the tone of its utterance, and which he takes to be the root of the Arabic tongue. }
\par\plain \f2\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain \f2\fs20\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f2\fs20\b0\i0 Farmers, say they, are inveterate grumblers. They have always grumbled. They grumbled in 1815, when the price of wheat was 80s.;\hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 and they give copious quotations from the evidence of practical agriculturists, taken by Parliamentary Committees, to prove their charge. The statement, and the intended inference from it, are based, however, upon an inexcusable ignorance, or a most criminal concealment of the facts. The unparalleled taxation and other expenses of the\~}{\f3\fs20\b0\i1 peculiar}{\f2\fs20\b0\i0 \~period referred to, rendered even the 80s. a poor remuneration to the home-grower. In 1815 the annual expenditure of the nation amounted to \loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2\uc1\u163\'A3100,000,000, while the population was only 13,000,000\hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 the rate of contribution being thus about \u163\'A37, 15s. per head. At present, the national expenditure is about \u163\'A352,000,000, and the population about 20,000,000, (Census 1841)\hich\f2 \emdash \loch\f2 that is, \u163\'A32, 12s. per head. The truth is, with wheat at 56s., we believe that the farmer of the present day would be better able to maintain his position than the farmer of 1815 was with wheat at 80s. It is marvellous to consider what unconscionable drafts the Free Trade sophists make upon the ignorance of their readers. Abolish taxation and lower wages to the Continental rate, and the energy and enterprise of the British farmer will enable him, even with his inferior climate, to compete with the agriculturists of more favoured climes.}{\f2\fs24\b0\i0  }
\par\plain \f2\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It seemed impossible that modern thought could house itself in such decrepit and superseded chambers.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
